{
  "attributes": {
    "Conventions": "CF-1.13, ACDD-1.3",
    "title": "All-sky camera raw sensor data",
    "summary": "Raw Bayer sensor counts from a fixed upward-looking all-sky (fisheye) camera, captured as five-exposure brackets placed on a regular 15-minute grid. The counts are cropped to the hemisphere disk and de-interleaved into the four Bayer channels, each stored as its own coherent half-resolution image. Alongside them: the per-exposure sensor metadata reported for every frame (analogue and digital gain, white-balance gains, colour correction matrix, sensor temperature, the exact capture instant), a demosaiced high-dynamic-range colour image for display, and the hemisphere disk mask that defines the crop. This is the raw store: no radiometric calibration is applied and no calibration-derived product is present. Viewing geometry, solid angle, the sky mask and radiance live in a separate next-level product.",
    "keywords": "EARTH SCIENCE > ATMOSPHERE > ATMOSPHERIC RADIATION > SOLAR RADIATION, EARTH SCIENCE > ATMOSPHERE > CLOUDS > CLOUD PROPERTIES, all-sky camera, sky imager, fisheye, raw sensor data, Bayer, HDR",
    "keywords_vocabulary": "GCMD Science Keywords",
    "source": "Sony IMX708 rolling-shutter CMOS sensor behind a fisheye lens, read out as a 10-bit BGGR Bayer mosaic in five-exposure brackets.",
    "processing_level": "Raw (Level 0). Analog-to-digital counts exactly as the sensor reported them; cropping and de-interleaving rearrange pixels but change no value. The HDR colour image is the one derived product, and it is a display product, not measurement data.",
